course: everyday
lesson: 433
title: Big kids, little kids and the truth at home
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Amelia in Bristol told her baby brother that the moon follows only him. He believed it for a year and waved at it every night. When he found out, he was a bit sad — then told the same thing to his little cousin, and it became a family joke. Kind make-believe with little ones is mostly fine, as long as it is never used to scare them or to get something out of them.
explain: Some big-kid tricks cross that line. Sam told his little sister that a swallowed watermelon seed grows a watermelon in your belly. She refused all fruit for a week and cried at night; Mum found out why and Sam had to explain the truth and felt mean. Kofi in London told his little brother «Mum said you have to give me your chocolate». The little one simply asked Mum. Kofi gave the chocolate back plus one of his own, and Mum said «Don't put words in my mouth, bruv.» Anyone can check «Mum said» by asking Mum. Ella's big sister said Ella could «have» her old phone, then a week later said she had only meant «borrow»; after a big row Mum made the rule clear for both: «give» means you keep it, «lend» means you give it back.
explain: Little kids can trick big ones too. Ivy kept saying she'd «tell Mum» things her big brother hadn't done unless he gave her his sweets, and mostly he gave in. One day he said «Go on then, tell her.» She did — and Mum believed him, not her. Threats to tell a lie stop when you tell a grown-up first. Jacob's big sister told him the ice-cream van «only plays music when it's run out» — their own mum had told her that when she was little, to save money. Jacob believed it until he saw kids queuing with cones. His sister laughed and bought him a cone to say sorry.
explain: Covering for a sibling drags you into their trouble. Charlie, nine, said his big sister was «in the shower» when she had sneaked out to a friend's; Dad heard no water and found her room empty, both got in trouble, and she didn't even say thanks. Two sisters in Dublin agreed to say they'd both done their chores. Mam asked them apart: «I did the dishes,» said one. «I did the dishes,» said the other. That was the end of the craic — Irish for fun — and they did every job in the house. Lies told by two people break when they are asked separately; the truth matches by itself.
explain: Some untruths are told out of love. Finn's family in Wales told his little sister their dog had «gone to live on a farm». Years later she found out it had died, and she was upset — not about the dog then, but about being told a story. They looked at old photos together. Most people, when they grow up, prefer the truth told gently. And when big brother Mo said «I'll never tell on you, and you never tell on me», then smashed a window, Leo quietly told Mum. Mo was furious, then calm, and later said Leo did the right thing.
fact: Gentle make-believe with little children, like the moon following them, is usually harmless if it never scares them.
fact: A «joke» that frightens a small child, especially at night, is no longer a joke.
fact: Swallowing a watermelon seed does not grow a watermelon inside you; the seed simply passes through.
fact: When someone says «Mum said», you can always check by asking Mum.
fact: Unclear promises cause rows; «give» means to keep, «lend» means to give back.
fact: Lying to cover for a sibling can make you part of their trouble; «I don't know, ask her» is honest.
fact: A threat to tell a lie about you loses its power when you tell a grown-up first.
fact: Two people telling the same lie often get caught when they are asked separately, because their details don't match.
fact: Kind lies, like «the dog went to a farm», may protect for a while, but most people later prefer the truth told gently.
fact: If what you see does not match what you are told, it is fine to ask.
fact: A promise to cover any lie is not a fair promise; you don't owe anyone silence about something serious.
fact: «Bruv» (London) means brother or mate; «craic» (Ireland) means fun or good times; «Mam» is Irish and northern English for Mum.
exercise: What did Amelia tell her baby brother about the moon? | That it follows only him | That it is made of cheese | That it is a big lamp in the sky | That it goes to sleep in the day
exercise: Why was Sam's watermelon-seed trick not a fair joke? | It frightened his little sister for a week | It was much too silly for anyone to believe | Watermelons are not real fruit | His sister didn't even like watermelon
exercise: What really happens if you swallow a watermelon seed? | It passes through and nothing grows | A small plant starts to grow in your tummy | It makes your belly ache for days | It slowly turns into a little stone
exercise: How did Kofi's little brother find out the truth? | He asked Mum directly | He read Mum's notes | Kofi told him later | His sister told him
exercise: What did Mum mean by «Don't put words in my mouth»? | Don't say I said things I didn't say | Don't eat with your mouth full at the table | Don't talk while I'm talking | Don't shout so loud indoors
exercise: Ella's sister said she could «have» her old phone, then said «borrow». What did Mum agree with them? | «Give» means keep, «lend» means give back | Phones can never be shared between sisters | The phone goes to Ella forever, whatever was said | Nobody gets the phone until they stop arguing
exercise: What did Charlie tell Dad when his sister sneaked out? | That she was in the shower | That she was at the shop | That she was asleep | That he didn't know
exercise: How did Dad see through Charlie's story? | He heard no water running | He saw her walking down the street | Her friend's mum rang to ask | The shower had been broken all week
exercise: How did Ivy's threats finally stop? | Her brother said «go on, tell her» and Mum believed him | Ivy just grew out of it after a year or so | Her brother gave her all his sweets to keep her quiet | Mum banned sweets
exercise: What did Finn's sister feel upset about when she grew up? | Being told a story instead of the truth | That the dog really lived on a farm far away | That she never had a dog | That Finn had taken the dog away
exercise: What did Jacob's sister say about the ice-cream van's music? | It only played when the van had run out | It played when the ice cream was free for kids | It played only for grown-ups | It played to warn everyone about rain
exercise: How did Jacob discover the truth about the van? | He saw kids queuing with cones | His mum told him | The driver told him | He read a sign on the van
exercise: How did the Dublin sisters' shared lie fall apart? | They both said «I did the dishes» | They both said nothing at all when asked | One owned up at once and said sorry | Mam saw them playing outside instead of cleaning
exercise: What did Mo ask Leo to promise? | Never to tell on each other | To do all his chores for him | To share his games | To keep the window clean
exercise: What did Mo say later about Leo telling? | Leo did the right thing | Leo was a traitor and a snitch | Leo should have lied for him | He never spoke to Leo again
exercise: Why does the truth «match by itself» when two people are asked apart? | They both remember what really happened | They practised it together the night before | They say it at the same time | Grown-ups only ask once
test: Big brother: «Mum said you gotta give me your sweets, bruv». What's the smart move for the little one? | Go and ask Mum | Hand them over at once | Hide them in the garden | Cry until he goes away
test: Sister: «if you swallow a seed a watermelon grows in your tummy». Little brother is crying. What's the truth to tell him? | The seed just passes through, nothing grows | Only some seeds grow, the black ones | It grows only if you drink lots of water after | It grows only at night
test: Ivy: «gimme your sweets or I'll tell Mum you hit me». He didn't. What's the best answer? | «Go on then» — and tell Mum about the threat himself | Give her the sweets every week so she stays quiet | Hit her so the story is true | Hide from Mum all day
test: Two kids agree to say «we both cleaned». What would happen if a parent asked them separately what they each did? | Their details might not match | Their stories would always match perfectly | Parents never ask separately | The cleaning would get done
test: Mam in Dublin: «That's the end of the craic.» What does she mean? | The fun is over | The window cracked | The chores are done | The dishes are broken
test: Dad: «Where's your sister?» Charlie knows she sneaked out. What's an honest thing to say? | «I don't know, ask her» or the truth | «In the shower, Dad» | «She's asleep upstairs, don't wake her» | «She went to Gran's for tea»
test: Jacob's sister bought him a cone after the ice-cream van joke. Why? | To say sorry for tricking him | Because the van was giving out free cones that day | Because Mum told her the joke was true | To trick him again the next day
test: Little sister finds out years later the dog didn't go to a farm. What helps most? | Talking honestly and looking at photos together | Saying it was a different dog that went away | Saying the farm was real | Never talking about it again
test: «moon follows only u, baby bro». Why is this joke mostly fine? | It's gentle and doesn't scare him | Because the moon really follows one person | Because babies can't understand | It isn't fine at all
test: Mo: «I never tell on you, you never tell on me». Then Mo breaks a window. Does Leo have to keep quiet? | No, that's not a fair promise about something serious | Yes, a promise is a promise always | Only if Mo pays him | Yes, brothers must always lie for each other, whatever happens
test: In a cartoon, a big sister always tricks her little brother into giving her his dessert. What is true in real life? | Tricking smaller kids is unfair and usually found out | It's a fair way for big kids to get a bit more food | Little kids enjoy being tricked | Parents always take the big one's side
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion «Mother said so» employed by an elder sibling to obtain a younger sibling's possessions. | It may be verified by applying to the mother, whereupon the falsehood is exposed | It is always to be accepted without question by the younger sibling | It is binding upon the younger sibling | It cannot be verified by any means available to a child
exam: Identify the principal reason for which two persons maintaining a shared falsehood are frequently detected when questioned separately. | The details of their accounts do not correspond | They are questioned at excessive length | Separate questioning is prohibited | Shared falsehoods are invariably true in every particular
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a falsehood which causes a young child to fear eating fruit? | It ceases to be a jest, as it frightens the child | It is harmless, being merely amusing to the elder child | It improves the child's diet | It is true in respect of certain fruits
exam: It is given that a child was told in kindness that a deceased pet had moved to a farm. Determine the response most commonly reported upon later discovery. | A preference for having been told the truth gently | Gratitude for the continued deception | Indifference to the matter | A wish that the deception had been maintained indefinitely
